---
// §9.7 Try It Yourself — plain-language (Krug/Redish), menu-first, teaser not a full
// copy of the README. Links out to the repo rather than duplicating setup steps.
// Human review (2026-07-23) added a Docker Desktop download link + InfoTip
// (short glossary popover) and BeginnersTrail guidance for readers who don't
// know what GitHub/cloning/a terminal are.
// Human review (2026-07-28): removed the git-free "Download ZIP" path. GitHub's
// ZIP archive has no .git folder, and docker-compose.yml bind-mounts .git into
// the monitor container so it can push the retrain trigger — a ZIP-downloaded
// folder silently breaks the automatic retraining demo (the container gets an
// empty .git dir, and every push fails forever with no visible error). git
// clone is now the one documented path, so Git joins Docker Desktop as a
// stated prerequisite.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import InfoTip from '../components/InfoTip.astro';
import BeginnersTrail from '../components/BeginnersTrail.astro';
import { REPO_URL, withBase } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Run it yourself — Preempt Analytics"
  description="Run the Preempt Analytics predictive-maintenance demo on your own machine. Two prerequisites (Docker Desktop and Git), a friendly menu, and a link to the full instructions on GitHub."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-16 md:px-8 md:py-24">
    <a href={withBase('/')} class="text-sm text-teal-400 hover:text-teal-300">← Back to home</a>

    <h1 class="mt-6 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-offwhite sm:text-5xl">
      Run Preempt Analytics on your own machine.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
      Everything runs inside Docker — no Python, no ML knowledge needed. Just
      two quick installs, then some waiting while things download.
    </p>

    <BeginnersTrail title="Never done anything like this before? Here's the whole path.">
      <p>You don't need to know how to code. The whole journey is three steps:</p>
      <p>
        <strong>1. Install Docker Desktop and Git</strong> (below) — two free
        apps, installed like any other program.<br />
        <strong>2. Get the code onto your computer</strong> — paste one
        command into a terminal (explained below).<br />
        <strong>3. Run the menu script</strong> and press a number — press
        <strong>6</strong> first to turn everything on, then any other
        number to try something.
      </p>
      <p>
        That's the whole thing — no Docker commands to memorize, just a
        couple of lines to paste and a number to press after that.
      </p>
    </BeginnersTrail>

    <!-- Prerequisites -->
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-navy-800/40 p-5">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-offwhite">You need two things, both free, both one-time installs:</p>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm text-muted">
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
            class="text-teal-300 underline decoration-teal-400/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-teal-200"
          >
            Docker Desktop
          </a>
          <InfoTip label="What is Docker Desktop?">
            Docker Desktop is a free app that runs this whole project in a
            self-contained box on your computer — you won't need to
            separately install Python, databases, or anything else by hand.
          </InfoTip>
          — install it and make sure it's running (the whale icon in your
          menu/task bar).
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://git-scm.com/downloads"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
            class="text-teal-300 underline decoration-teal-400/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-teal-200"
          >
            Git
          </a>
          <InfoTip label="What is Git?">
            Git is the tool that downloads ("clones") a project's full
            history onto your computer. Macs often offer to install it
            automatically the first time you type a git command in
            Terminal — just follow the prompt.
          </InfoTip>
          — used once, to get the project folder onto your computer.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Get the code: git clone is now the one documented path (human review,
         2026-07-28 — see the frontmatter note: a ZIP download has no .git
         folder, which silently breaks the automatic-retraining demo). -->
    <h2 class="mt-16 text-xl font-semibold text-offwhite">Get the code onto your computer</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">Open a terminal and paste this:</p>
    <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-navy-950 p-4">
      <pre class="text-sm text-teal-200"><code>git clone https://github.com/Preempt-Analytics-Demo/predictive-maintenance-demo.git
cd predictive-maintenance-demo</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
      That folder is your project folder for everything below. Prefer to
      browse first?{' '}
      <a href={REPO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-teal-400 hover:text-teal-300">
        View the source on GitHub →
      </a>
    </p>

    <BeginnersTrail title="What's GitHub? What does &ldquo;cloning&rdquo; mean?">
      <p>
        <strong>GitHub</strong> is a website where code is stored and shared —
        think of it like a shared drive, but for software projects.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Cloning</strong> just means downloading a full copy of that
        project onto your own computer — the <code>git clone</code> command
        above does exactly that.
      </p>
      <p>
        A <strong>terminal</strong> (or "command line") is a text-based way of
        giving your computer instructions. Paste the two lines above in, one
        at a time, and press Enter after each.
      </p>
    </BeginnersTrail>

    <!-- Friendly path first -->
    <h2 class="mt-16 text-xl font-semibold text-offwhite">The easy way — a menu, no commands to remember</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      Once you have the project folder on your computer (see above), open a
      terminal in that folder and run one script below. It replaces every
      Docker command with a numbered menu.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-navy-950 p-4">
      <pre class="text-sm text-teal-200"><code># Mac / Linux
./preempt.sh

# Windows
.\preempt.ps1</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
      <strong class="text-offwhite">Press 6 first</strong> — that turns on
      the background services (once per session is enough). After that,
      press 1 or 2 to simulate sensor readings, 3 to open the drift report,
      or 4/5 to watch things happen live.
    </p>

    <!-- Manual path second -->
    <h2 class="mt-16 text-xl font-semibold text-offwhite">Prefer to type it? Three commands</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      This replaces everything above — get the code and start the stack in
      one go, no menu needed.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-navy-950 p-4">
      <pre class="text-sm text-teal-200"><code>git clone https://github.com/Preempt-Analytics-Demo/predictive-maintenance-demo.git
cd predictive-maintenance-demo
docker compose up -d</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
      From there you can generate sensor readings and watch drift detection trigger
      an automatic retraining run — the full walkthrough lives in the README.
    </p>

    <!-- Shared-resource expectation -->
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-teal-400/20 bg-teal-400/[0.04] p-5">
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">
        <span class="font-semibold text-teal-300">Heads up:</span> retraining runs on a
        shared demo pipeline (about 10 runs/hour for everyone combined). If that limit's
        been hit, your data still uploads — this run's retraining is skipped rather than
        queued, and the limit frees up again on a rolling basis. Nothing is broken.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12">
      <a
        href={REPO_URL}
        rel="noopener"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg bg-teal-400 px-6 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-navy-950 transition-colors hover:bg-teal-300"
      >
        Full instructions on GitHub →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
